valid or unreadable input. Week-date results are checked against Python's own `date.fromisocalendar` for 40 years, including the tricky weeks at year boundaries. A month-length helper is checked against the standard library for 220 years.\n- **Time parsing:** all formats, both `,` and `.` as the decimal mark, fractions of hours and minutes, extra decimal places being cut off rather than rounded, all time-zone styles, and malformed or out-of-range values.\n- **Combined date-times:** parsing with and without a time zone, plus the error when the `T` is missing or appears twice.\n- **Time zones:** reading offsets, the fixed-offset and UTC zones (including that UTC survives being saved and reloaded with `pickle`), sanity checks on the local time zone, formatting offsets three ways, and the errors for an unknown format and an offset over 99 hours.\n- **Formatting:** every predefined date, time and date-time format, zero-padding, longer years, and checks that formatting then re-parsing gives back the original.\n- **Duration parsing and formatting:** plain durations come back as `timedelta` and ones with years/months as `Duration`; fractions, signs, the alternative `P0001-02-03T04:05:06` form, invalid strings and non-string input. Formatting includes negatives, fractional seconds, the weeks format and round-trips.\n- **The `Duration` class:** equality (including against `timedelta`), hashing, `pickle`, negation, adding, subtracting and multiplying, and adding to dates, including month-end clamping (Jan 31 + 1 month = Feb 29 in leap years) and leap-day handling. Also the error for fractional months on dates, and conversion to a `timedelta` from a start or end date.\n\n**One library quirk:** `parse_date(\"1985-00-10\")` returns 1985-01-10 instead of raising an error, because the parser treats month `00` as \"use the default month\" (`isodate/isodates.py:186`). My first run failed on this case. Since I can't change the library, I swapped that input for another invalid date (`1